(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-cms-exam-6-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 6 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In a patient with a post-bronchodilator ratio below 0.70, a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 42% predicted corresponds to which airflow grade?","choices":["Grade 1 (mild)","Grade 2 (moderate)","Grade 3 (severe)","Grade 4 (very severe)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Grade 3, severe, covers a post-bronchodilator 1-second volume of 30% up to 50% predicted, so 42% falls there. Grade 2 starts at 50% and grade 4 is below 30%. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which spirometry pattern before a bronchodilator signals airflow limitation in asthma?","choices":["Reduced 1-second volume and reduced ratio","Normal ratio, reduced vital capacity","Increased 1-second volume","Normal values, lower after dilator"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Before a bronchodilator, airflow limitation shows as a reduced forced expiratory volume in 1 second and a reduced ratio of that volume to forced vital capacity. Improvement after the bronchodilator then supports asthma. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman on home oxygen has dry, cracked lips and uses a petroleum jelly lip balm several times a day. Which teaching applies?","choices":["Switch her oxygen to a non-rebreather","Stop using petroleum-based products","Use more balm to protect the lips","Stop her home oxygen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Petroleum-based products such as lip balms, lotions, oils and grease are to be avoided with home oxygen. Her petroleum jelly lip balm, used several times a day, is exactly that, so she should stop it. Her dry lips are not a reason to keep using it. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 7, 15"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has a myocardial infarction and is placed on oxygen in the ambulance although his saturation was 98%. Which effect of oxygen makes this a problem?","choices":["Vasoconstriction","Carbon dioxide retention","Mucosal irritation","Fire risk"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His saturation was 98%, so he was not hypoxic. Oxygen is not given in a myocardial infarction unless the saturation is below 91%, because oxygen causes vasoconstriction. Giving it to a man with a normal saturation exposes him to that effect without benefit. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 9"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man has daily asthma symptoms and uses albuterol daily, but he wakes at night only once a month and has a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 85% predicted. He has some limitation playing pickup basketball. Which severity classification fits?","choices":["Mild persistent","Moderate persistent","Intermittent","Severe persistent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Daily symptoms, daily short-acting beta-2 agonist use and some limitation of activity are moderate persistent features. Even though his night waking and 85% function are milder, severity is assigned to the most severe category in which any feature occurs. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man who drank methanol has a bicarbonate of 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L) and a carbon dioxide of 29 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). By the Winter formula, what does his carbon dioxide show?","choices":["An added respiratory acidosis","An added respiratory alkalosis","Impending respiratory failure","Appropriate compensation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: 1.5 x 14 + 8 = 29, plus or minus 2, so 27 to 31. His 29 sits inside that range, so the fall in carbon dioxide is the expected respiratory compensation for his elevated-gap metabolic acidosis from methanol, with no second disorder. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 55, 58, 59"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy has influenza with a temperature of 102.8°F and body aches. His parent wants to give him a fever medicine from the cabinet at home. Which medication should be avoided?","choices":["Acetaminophen","Ibuprofen","Aspirin","Oral fluids and rest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin is avoided in children younger than 18 years with influenza because it is associated with Reye syndrome. Acetaminophen or ibuprofen is used for fever as part of supportive care, along with rest and fluids. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man has 3 days of fever to 102.6°F, shaking chills, a cough with purulent sputum and a stabbing right-sided chest pain on breathing. His respiratory rate is 28/min. Over the right lower lobe there are crackles and dullness to percussion. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Community-acquired pneumonia","Aspiration pneumonitis","Acute bronchitis","Lung abscess"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High fever, chills, purulent productive cough, pleuritic chest pain and tachypnea are more severe than a cold or bronchitis, and the findings sit over one lobe: crackles and dullness to percussion. Acquired outside the hospital, this is community-acquired pneumonia. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 16, 23, 27, 28, 50, 51, 53"},{"q":"Using about 2 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg acutely and 5 per 10 chronically, which bicarbonate fits acute respiratory alkalosis with a carbon dioxide of 30 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44, midpoint 40) from a baseline bicarbonate of 24?","choices":["19 mEq/L","22 mEq/L","24 mEq/L","26 mEq/L"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The carbon dioxide fell 10 mm Hg (40 to 30), and acute respiratory alkalosis lowers bicarbonate about 2 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg. So 24 - 2 = 22 mEq/L is the expected bicarbonate. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 42-year-old poultry farmer has fever, a nonproductive cough, severe headache and a unilateral lower-lobe infiltrate. Psittacosis is suspected. Which test is most useful for timely confirmation?","choices":["Respiratory nucleic acid test","Blood count and liver enzymes","A single antibody titer","Routine sputum culture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A respiratory nucleic acid amplification test, such as polymerase chain reaction, is timely and sensitive for Chlamydia psittaci, coordinated with public health laboratories. His poultry exposure and the lower-lobe infiltrate make that the confirming test. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 45% predicted, a 6-minute walk distance of 200 meters, a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 3, and a body mass index of 20. What is his BODE (body mass index, obstruction, dyspnea, exercise) index score?","choices":["5","6","7","8"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. 1-second volume 45% (36 to 49) = 2 + walk 200 meters (150 to 249) = 2 + dyspnea grade 3 = 2 + body mass index 20 (21 or less) = 1, for a total of 7. A score of 7 to 10 predicts about 18% 4-year survival. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with confirmed chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptom assessment test score of 6 and a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 1. He had 2 moderate exacerbations treated at home in the past year. Under the current three-group scheme, which group is he in?","choices":["Group C","Group B","Group E","Group A"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two moderate exacerbations in the past year make him high risk, and any high-risk patient is group E regardless of symptoms. His low symptom score of 6 does not change that; group E starts on a long-acting beta agonist plus a long-acting antimuscarinic. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 40, 41"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man tore down an old barn where bats roosted. Six days later he has high fever, chills, myalgias and a nonproductive cough. An early urine Histoplasma antigen is negative, but suspicion remains high. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Amphotericin-based induction","Serum cryptococcal antigen","Repeat antigen testing","Histoplasmosis is excluded"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Early negative tests do not exclude histoplasmosis. When suspicion persists, testing is repeated or respiratory specimens are obtained. His bat exposure and typical illness keep suspicion high, so one early negative result does not end the workup. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with pneumonia has an arterial blood gas before and after starting oxygen. After oxygen, his arterial oxygen pressure is higher, his carbon dioxide is unchanged, and his pH has returned toward normal. How is this response read?","choices":["A response if saturation reaches 100%","Carbon dioxide retention","No response","A good response"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A good response is an increase in arterial oxygen pressure without an increase in arterial carbon dioxide, plus correction of the pH. His oxygen pressure rose, his carbon dioxide stayed unchanged, and his pH moved back toward normal, which meets all three. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with a metabolic alkalosis from repeated vomiting has generalized weakness and hyporeflexia. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.52 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 48 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 38 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which laboratory finding best explains her weakness and hyporeflexia?","choices":["High carbon dioxide","High potassium","Low potassium","Low bicarbonate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Metabolic alkalosis brings hypokalemia (potassium shifts into cells, and vomiting adds losses), and the associated hypokalemia causes weakness and hyporeflexia. Her alkalemia at 7.52 fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman has mild breathlessness she attributes to deconditioning. Post-bronchodilator spirometry shows a ratio of 0.68 with a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 85% predicted. Which consideration applies before labeling her with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["Any ratio below 0.70 settles it","She needs triple inhaled therapy","She needs alpha-1 antitrypsin testing","The fixed 0.70 ratio may overdiagnose"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In adults 65 and older, the fixed 0.70 ratio may overdiagnose, so clinical context is weighed. A borderline ratio with near-normal airflow and mild symptoms in an 84-year-old calls for that judgment. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a resting oxygen saturation of 85% on room air on 2 visits, with no cor pulmonale (right heart failure). Which treatment is indicated?","choices":["A sleep study for apnea","Long-term oxygen for over 15 hours a day","No oxygen until saturation falls below 80%","Oxygen just during exercise"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Severe resting desaturation, a saturation of 88% or less at rest, qualifies for long-term oxygen therapy for more than 15 hours a day. Oxygen for severe resting hypoxemia decreases the risk of death, prolongs life and improves quality of life. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 63, 64, 66"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman at 30 weeks of pregnancy has a routine arterial blood gas for mild breathlessness: pH 7.46 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 28 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 19 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Respiratory acidosis","Chronic respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Acute respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.46 is alkalemia, and the carbon dioxide of 28 is low and matches it: respiratory alkalosis. Bicarbonate fell about 5 per 10 mm Hg (12 below 40 gives about 18 to 19), a chronic renal response. Pregnancy is a listed endocrine cause. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What risk of death does a CURB-65 (confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure, age 65 or older) total of 2 carry?","choices":["Under 3%","About 9%","15 to 40%","About 27%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A total of 2 points is moderate severity, with a risk of death of about 9%, and the pathway sends these patients to the hospital. A total of 0 to 1 is low severity (under 3%), and 3 to 5 is high severity (15 to 40%). — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 31, 29"},{"q":"What is the primary risk factor for chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Burning biomass for cooking","Sedentary lifestyle","Tobacco smoking or smoke exposure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tobacco smoking or exposure is the primary risk factor, behind about 90% of cases. Secondary risks are biomass burning, occupational dusts and fumes, a sedentary lifestyle and, much more rarely, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Why can a mycobacterial culture take weeks to return a result?","choices":["The smear must turn negative","Three specimens are pooled first","Molecular tests must run first","The organism grows slowly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mycobacterium tuberculosis grows slowly, so cultures can take weeks. That is why a rapid molecular test is run on at least one respiratory specimen alongside the smear and culture: molecular tests can give results quickly. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 19-year-old college student has had episodes of wheezing, chest tightness and cough for 2 years, worse at night and after cleaning his dusty dorm room. He has allergic rhinitis and atopic dermatitis (eczema). Between episodes his lung exam is normal. Spirometry shows a reduced forced expiratory volume in 1 second that improves 18% after albuterol. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":["Intrinsic asthma","Vocal fold dysfunction","Extrinsic asthma","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Episodic wheeze, chest tightness and cough worse at night, a dust trigger, allergic rhinitis and eczema (the atopic triad), a normal exam between episodes, and an 18% rise in the 1-second volume after albuterol (12% or more is a positive response) make allergic, extrinsic asthma the diagnosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 25, 35, 53"},{"q":"A 77-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and osteoporosis takes triple inhaled therapy. She has had 2 pneumonias this year and a wrist fracture after a fall. Which component of her regimen is linked to both pneumonia and fracture risk?","choices":["The inhaled corticosteroid","The long-acting beta agonist","The long-acting antimuscarinic","Her as-needed albuterol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled corticosteroids increase the risk of pneumonia, and in older adults the concerns include inhaled corticosteroid-related pneumonia and fracture risk. Her repeated pneumonias and fracture make that component the one to reassess. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 42, 53"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with nonsevere community-acquired pneumonia treated as an outpatient is afebrile, eating and clinically stable on day 3. What is the shortest total antibiotic course that may be appropriate for her?","choices":["3 days","5 days","7 days","10 days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For clinically stable, nonsevere community-acquired pneumonia, a course shorter than 5 days, with a minimum of 3 days, may be appropriate. She is stable by day 3. Severe pneumonia is different: at least 5 days and until clinical stability. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 45, 47"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with known hypercalcemia has a metabolic alkalosis, a high urine chloride and a normal blood pressure. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.47 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 45 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 32 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). In which group of metabolic alkalosis does she belong?","choices":["Saline responsive","Saline unresponsive, normotensive","Saline unresponsive, hypertensive","Post-hypercapnic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A high urine chloride (above 40 mEq/L) marks saline-unresponsive alkalosis; with a normal blood pressure it is the normotensive group, which lists hypoparathyroidism and hypercalcemia and severe potassium depletion. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 28, 41, 42, 43"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman has coronavirus disease 2019 with fever, cough and mild shortness of breath. Her oxygen saturation is 96% on room air and her respiratory rate is 20/min, and a chest radiograph shows a patchy infiltrate. Which severity category fits?","choices":["Mild illness","Moderate illness","Severe illness","Critical illness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Moderate illness has symptoms with a positive chest radiograph and an oxygen saturation of at least 94% on room air. Her patchy infiltrate makes her more than mild, and her saturation of 96% with a normal respiratory rate keeps her below severe. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has 12 mm of induration on a tuberculin skin test. His chest radiograph shows a fibrotic upper-lobe lesion, he does not recall ever being treated for tuberculosis, and he has no symptoms. Which step must come before latent-infection treatment?","choices":["An interferon-gamma release assay","A repeat skin test in 8 to 10 weeks","A second chest radiograph in 1 year","Exclusion of active tuberculosis disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fibrotic lesions warrant review of prior treatment and exclusion of active disease, and tuberculosis disease must be excluded before latent-infection treatment begins. Imaging cannot reliably determine activity, so microbiologic testing answers that question. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 27, 29, 37"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and heart failure is hospitalized on day 5 of influenza, confirmed by molecular testing, with hypoxemia. Which antiviral is most appropriate?","choices":["Amantadine","Inhaled zanamivir","Single-dose baloxavir","Oral or enteric oseltamivir"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hospitalized patients generally receive oral or enteric oseltamivir, which is preferred for hospitalized, severe and high-risk patients and is started even after 48 hours. His age, lung and heart disease and admission all point to it, with renal dose adjustment. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 97, 98, 108, 109"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with severe community-acquired pneumonia in the intensive care unit had methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus isolated from her sputum last year. She has been started on ceftriaxone plus azithromycin. Which agent should be added?","choices":["Moxifloxacin","Clindamycin","Ertapenem","Vancomycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus coverage is added only for validated risks, and a prior respiratory isolate is one. The agents named for it are vancomycin or linezolid, added to the beta-lactam combination; cultures are obtained and therapy de-escalated as results return. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 40, 44"},{"q":"On an arterial blood gas, an arterial oxygen pressure at or below what cutoff qualifies a patient for home oxygen with no comorbidity required?","choices":["50 mm Hg","55 mm Hg","59 mm Hg","65 mm Hg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An arterial oxygen pressure at or below 55 mm Hg qualifies for home oxygen without any comorbidity (Group I). Readings of 56 to 59 mm Hg qualify only when pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale or polycythemia is documented. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man with asthma and a body mass index of 36 asks how his weight relates to his asthma. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Obesity makes asthma harder to control","Obesity makes asthma milder","Obesity matters just for exercise-induced symptoms","Obesity has no link to asthma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asthma is more frequent in people with a body mass index above 30, and their symptoms are more difficult to control, although the mechanism is unknown. His body mass index of 36 places him in that group. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with severe persistent asthma has needed 4 courses of oral corticosteroids this year despite confirmed adherence and good technique with her inhaled corticosteroid and long-acting beta-2 agonist. Which step is most appropriate?","choices":["Stop the long-acting beta-2 agonist","Start allergen immunotherapy at home","Refer to a lung or allergy specialist","Add theophylline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With adherence and technique confirmed, her repeated steroid-requiring exacerbations make her asthma refractory. Monoclonal antibody agents are used by pulmonology and allergy specialists and reduced the need for corticosteroids in trials, so referral to a specialist fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 84, 91"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman is expected to need mechanical ventilation for several days after a large stroke. Which measure helps prevent ventilator-associated pneumonia?","choices":["Keeping her lying flat","Elevating the head of the bed","Daily ventilator circuit changes","Prophylactic broad antibiotics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prevention is the main strategy for ventilator-associated pneumonia: early extubation, hand washing and sterile technique, limiting circuit changes, proper positioning with the head of the bed elevated, and supraglottic secretion drainage with mouth care. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 48, 49"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has had 9 days of coughing fits that end in vomiting, with a whooping sound on inspiration. Her nephew was diagnosed with pertussis last week. Her vital signs and lung examination are normal. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Supportive care and reassurance","Targeted pertussis testing","Chest radiograph","Reassess after 4 weeks of cough"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Paroxysmal cough, post-tussive vomiting, a whoop and a relevant exposure are the pertussis clues, and suspected pertussis needs targeted testing and treatment to reduce transmission. There is no need to wait for 4 weeks of cough before testing. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 15, 17, 18"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with hypertension treated with a thiazide diuretic is light-headed on standing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.49 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 46 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 34 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); potassium 3.2 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L); urine chloride is low. Along with isotonic saline, which medication change is indicated?","choices":["Start a mineralocorticoid","Stop the thiazide diuretic","Start a calcium carbonate antacid","Add a second diuretic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low urine chloride and orthostatic symptoms mark a saline-responsive metabolic alkalosis, and diuretic therapy is a listed cause. Treatment is isotonic saline for the volume deficit and discontinuing the diuretic. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man describes cough and chest tightness with cold air and smoke over several months. His spirometry is normal, with no meaningful change after albuterol. Asthma is still suspected. Which test is most appropriate next?","choices":["Chest x-ray","Repeat spirometry in 1 year","Methacholine challenge","Exercise challenge"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bronchial provocation testing with methacholine or histamine is useful when spirometry is not diagnostic, as here, where a typical history meets normal spirometry. His normal lung function also clears the rule that provocation is not done below 65% predicted. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man moved 1 year ago from an area with high tuberculosis incidence. He has 6 weeks of productive cough, fever, night sweats, weight loss and hemoptysis, and his chest radiograph shows an upper-lobe cavity. His interferon-gamma release assay is positive. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nontuberculous mycobacterial disease","Lung abscess","Pulmonary tuberculosis","Lung cancer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Residence in a high-incidence area is an exposure risk, and persistent cough, fever, night sweats, weight loss and hemoptysis are pulmonary manifestations; post-primary disease often involves the upper lobes and may cavitate. Imaging and the blood test support the suspicion; sputum microbiology confirms it. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 14, 16, 18, 28"},{"q":"What happens to carbon dioxide when ventilation increases?","choices":["It decreases","It stays the same","It turns into bicarbonate","It increases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Increased ventilation decreases carbon dioxide, and decreased ventilation increases it. In the steady state, carbon dioxide production matches excretion, so changing ventilation shifts the balance. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 12, 22, 30"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with group B chronic obstructive pulmonary disease still smokes a pack a day. She asks what single step would do the most to slow her disease. Which intervention is most appropriate?","choices":["Long-term azithromycin","Adding an inhaled corticosteroid","Smoking cessation with counseling","A chest computed tomography scan"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Smoking cessation is the only treatment that can slow progression, and every attempt should be combined with counseling to increase efficacy; nicotine replacement, bupropion or varenicline can be added. It is essential for every group. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 43, 46"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with asthma is reviewing his written action plan with you. Which finding is a danger sign telling him to take his quick-relief medicine and go to the hospital or call an ambulance now?","choices":["Mild wheeze with the first signs of a cold","Coughing at night","Peak flow at 75% of personal best","Blue lips or fingernails"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The action plan's danger signs are trouble walking and talking because of shortness of breath, and lips or fingernails that are blue. Either one means take the quick-relief medicine and go to the hospital or call an ambulance now, without waiting. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 88, 89"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man is about to start a tumor necrosis factor inhibitor. He has no symptoms of tuberculosis and has never been tested for it. Which step regarding tuberculosis is most appropriate before he starts?","choices":["Three sputum specimens for culture","Targeted testing for tuberculosis infection","No testing because he has no symptoms","Empiric latent-infection treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tumor necrosis factor inhibitors raise the risk of progression after tuberculosis infection, and testing is targeted at people at increased risk. A skin test or interferon-gamma release assay detects the immune response to infection; a positive result then needs clinical evaluation and chest imaging. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 15, 19"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with frequent exacerbations asks about taking azithromycin every day to prevent them, as a friend does. Which statement about long-term azithromycin is accurate?","choices":["It is routine first-line therapy","It replaces her inhalers","It has no side effects","It is rare and raises resistance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Some patients are put on long-term azithromycin or erythromycin, but this is rare, and it increases the risk of resistance and side effects. Frequent exacerbations despite optimal therapy are a reason for pulmonology referral. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 54, 59"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman taking theophylline for asthma has nausea, vomiting, headache and a rapid irregular heartbeat. Her potassium is low and her glucose high. Which explanation best fits her presentation?","choices":["Theophylline toxicity","Inhaled corticosteroid side effects","Aspirin-sensitive reaction","Albuterol side effects"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Theophylline has a narrow toxic-therapeutic range, and its toxic effects are nausea, vomiting, tachyarrhythmias, headache, seizure, hyperglycemia and hypokalemia, matching this woman's picture. That toxicity is why it is rarely used and needs close monitoring. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 71, 75, 81, 82, 92"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with diabetes and an acute myocardial infarction has an oxygen saturation of 87% on room air. Which is the right approach to oxygen?","choices":["Give oxygen, since saturation is below 91%","Withhold oxygen because of her diabetes","Wait for an arterial blood gas first","Withhold oxygen because of vasoconstriction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her saturation of 87% is below the 91% cutoff. Oxygen is held back in a myocardial infarction because of vasoconstriction, but only while the saturation is 91% or higher. She is hypoxic, so the benefit of correcting hypoxia wins and she should receive oxygen. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145), chloride 114 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106) and bicarbonate 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26). What is the calculated anion gap?","choices":["10","14","24","38"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anion gap = 138 - (114 + 14) = 138 - 128 = 10, within the normal 9 to 15. With a low bicarbonate and a high chloride, this is a normal-gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"For an eligible adult who has never had a pneumococcal conjugate vaccine, which vaccine type completes the series with one dose?","choices":["A 15-valent conjugate vaccine","A 23-valent polysaccharide vaccine","A 23-valent conjugate vaccine","A 20- or 21-valent conjugate vaccine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. One dose of the 20-valent or 21-valent pneumococcal conjugate vaccine completes the series for a conjugate-naive eligible adult. The 15-valent conjugate vaccine, by contrast, usually needs the 23-valent polysaccharide vaccine afterward. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 8, 9, 124"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman started an inhaled corticosteroid for moderate persistent asthma 1 week ago and wants to stop it because she does not feel much different yet. What should she be told?","choices":["It is meant to be used during attacks","It should work within minutes","Its full effect can take months","Her lack of change means she needs oral steroids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled corticosteroids are the preferred first-line treatment for persistent asthma, and most patients respond to twice-daily dosing, but the maximum response may take months. One week is too early to judge, and stopping would leave her inflammation untreated. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which examination finding correlates with the degree of airway obstruction in asthma?","choices":["Inspiratory crackles at the bases","A shortened expiratory phase","A prolonged expiratory phase","A pericardial friction rub"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A prolonged expiratory phase during normal respiration correlates with obstruction, because air takes longer to leave through narrowed airways. In severe exacerbations wheezing may disappear, leaving decreased breath sounds with prolonged expiration. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man with human immunodeficiency virus infection has never had Pneumocystis pneumonia. His CD4 (cluster of differentiation 4) count is 160 cells/mm³, and his viral load is detectable. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start primary prophylaxis","Prophylaxis after a first episode","Wait until the count is below 100","No prophylaxis while he feels well"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Primary prophylaxis starts for a CD4 (cluster of differentiation 4) count below 100 cells/mm³, or a count of 100 to 200 with a detectable viral load. His 160 with a detectable viral load meets the second criterion, and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is the preferred drug. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman develops an acute respiratory acidosis after aspiration pneumonitis two hours ago. When will her kidneys begin to compensate?","choices":["In 6 to 12 hours, completing over days","Within minutes, completing in an hour","They do not; the lungs compensate instead","After weeks, completing over months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In respiratory acidosis the kidneys excrete acid as ammonium chloride and retain bicarbonate; this begins 6 to 12 hours after onset, and complete compensation may take days. At two hours her bicarbonate is near normal. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 17-year-old runner with exercise-induced bronchospasm uses albuterol before every practice, yet still has frequent symptoms during training. Which change is most likely to prevent his episodes?","choices":["Regular inhaled corticosteroid use","Stopping exercise permanently","Saving albuterol for after symptoms start","Cromolyn sodium taken during symptoms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Exercise-induced bronchospasm is treated with a short-acting beta-2 agonist before exercise but is best prevented with regular inhaled corticosteroid use. His frequent symptoms despite pre-exercise albuterol call for the regular controller. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 66, 96"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man is found unresponsive and is not breathing. He has a pulse. A colleague reaches for a non-rebreather mask. Why does that device not fit his situation?","choices":["Its fraction of oxygen is too low","Its bag must collapse on each breath","It is a high flow oxygen device","It is for patients ventilating well"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. He is unresponsive and not breathing, though he has a pulse. A non-rebreather mask is used in severely hypoxic patients who are still ventilating well, and he is not breathing at all, so the mask's listed use does not fit him. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 15, 16, 17, 20"},{"q":"A 75-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a severe exacerbation. She is confused, her blood pressure is falling despite fluids, and she is tiring and may need invasive ventilation. Which level of care is most appropriate?","choices":["Outpatient management","Intensive care admission","Hospital ward admission","Pulmonary rehabilitation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Intensive care admission is for patients who are hemodynamically unstable, have a major infection or need invasive ventilatory support, and for sepsis, altered mental status, respiratory failure or respiratory acidosis. Her confusion, falling pressure and possible need for intubation meet these. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man who smokes has coughed up thick sputum most days for 4 months of each of the last 3 years. He has rhonchi, bluish lips and swelling of both ankles. His blood gas shows low oxygen and raised carbon dioxide, and his diffusing capacity is near normal. Post-bronchodilator spirometry shows a ratio of 0.64. Which subtype best describes his disease?","choices":["Emphysema subtype","Chronic bronchitis without obstruction","Asthma","Chronic bronchitis subtype"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Productive cough for at least 3 months a year over 2 years, rhonchi, cyanosis, edema, early hypoxemia and hypercapnia, and a near-normal diffusing capacity, with confirmed obstruction (ratio 0.64), are the chronic bronchitis picture (the blue bloater). — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 6, 14, 15"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman who works in a setting with ongoing tuberculosis transmission has a positive interferon-gamma release assay on targeted testing. She has no symptoms, her examination is normal and her chest radiograph is normal. Which best describes her tuberculosis status?","choices":["Latent tuberculosis infection","Pulmonary tuberculosis disease","Reactivation (secondary) tuberculosis","Progressive primary tuberculosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A positive interferon-gamma release assay shows infection, and her clinical evaluation found no evidence of disease: no symptoms, a normal examination and a normal radiograph, which is usual in latent infection. Latent infection is asymptomatic and not infectious. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 10, 19, 29"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has resistant hypertension, muscle weakness and a potassium of 2.8 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.49 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 34 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.49 is alkalemia, and the bicarbonate of 34 is high and matches it, so it is metabolic. The carbon dioxide of 47 is slight respiratory compensation. Hypertension with hypokalemia points to a hypertensive saline-unresponsive cause such as primary aldosteronism. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with ventilator-associated pneumonia has had an adequate response to culture-directed antibiotics. What is the usual total duration of therapy?","choices":["3 days","5 days","7 days","14 days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia, a 7-day course is usual when the response is adequate, adjusted to clinical response, with prompt de-escalation as cultures clarify the diagnosis. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 46, 49"},{"q":"In an older adult, which factor most raises the risk of aspiration pneumonia?","choices":["Cruise ship travel","Bat or bird droppings","Pet bird exposure","Dysphagia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Risk factors for aspiration include dysphagia, altered consciousness and impaired airway protection; in the older patient, a swallowed bolus can enter the trachea. Swallowing, oral hygiene, positioning and reversible risks are assessed. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman without asthma or chronic lung disease has 8 days of cough after a cold, with a mild expiratory wheeze. She is afebrile with a normal oxygen saturation and no focal findings. Which treatment should not be prescribed routinely?","choices":["A short bronchodilator trial","Oral corticosteroids","Acetaminophen as needed","Counseling on cough duration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Systemic or inhaled corticosteroids are not routinely prescribed for uncomplicated bronchitis. Her wheeze is the transient wheeze of bronchitis from inflammation and mucus, with no asthma history and no focal findings, so a steroid adds risk without benefit. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 15, 17, 19, 20"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease was intubated with a carbon dioxide of 78 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), and the ventilator brought it to 38 within a few hours. He has had no vomiting, nasogastric suction, diuretics or antacids. The next morning: pH 7.55 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 38 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 32 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What best explains his alkalemia?","choices":["Worsening chronic respiratory acidosis","Acute respiratory alkalosis","Renal compensation that overshot","Post-hypercapnic metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His kidneys had retained bicarbonate for his chronic hypercapnia; when the carbon dioxide was lowered quickly, that bicarbonate of 32 was left unopposed, a post-hypercapnic metabolic alkalosis. This is why rapid, aggressive carbon dioxide reduction is avoided in chronic respiratory acidosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"}]};
